;id&quot;:&quot;technology/technology&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Technology&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;technology/internet&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Internet&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;world/france&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;France&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;world/iran&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Iran&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;world/israel&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Israel&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;world/europe-news&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Europe&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;campaign/email/headlines-europe&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Campaign&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Headlines Europe&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;type/article&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Type&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Article&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;tone/analysis&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Tone&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Analysis&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;profile/danmilmo&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Contributor&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Dan Milmo&quot;,&quot;bylineImageUrl&quot;:&quot;https://i.guim.co.uk/img/uploads/2022/08/09/Dan_Milmo.jpg?width=300&amp;quality=85&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=max&amp;s=d930eec555a9d63be6ed1a312ca4e1d0&quot;,&quot;bylineLargeImageUrl&quot;:&quot;https://i.guim.co.uk/img/uploads/2022/08/09/Dan_Milmo,_R.png?width=300&amp;quality=85&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=max&amp;s=9df7faf39f71b4987ccb1b6b1fced435&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;tracking/commissioningdesk/uk-foreign&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Tracking&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;UK Foreign&quot;}]}"></gu-island><main class="dcr-0"><div id="article"><article class="dcr-t7zu1o"><div class="dcr-16h1akv"><div><div class="dcr-by5l9z"><figure id="aac0a85f-70e2-42ce-ba97-74a90dc2013a" class="dcr-7jat1q"><div id="img-1" class="dcr-1t7hdmw"><span class="dcr-kogzpx"><div class="dcr-18mnkpm"><div class="dcr-1xj58uj"><label for="the-checkbox" class="dcr-4sl2vt"></label><input type="checkbox" id="the-checkbox"/> <div id="the-caption"><figcaption class="dcr-1mfxiaj"><span class="dcr-13i4yc9"></span><span class="dcr-1cipnsy">Empty refrigerators at an Intermarche supermarket in Paris the day after the cyberattack.</span> Photograph: Lucien Libert/Reuters</figcaption></div></div></div></span><a href="#img-1" class="open-lightbox dcr-zwabc0"><button type="button" class="open-lightbox dcr-1bitgpn"><span class="dcr-1shbixy">View image in fullscreen</span></button></a></div><span class="dcr-qda0hp"><figcaption class="dcr-1e9iiy9"><span class="dcr-13i4yc9"></span><span class="dcr-1cipnsy">Empty refrigerators at an Intermarche supermarket in Paris the day after the cyberattack.</span> Photograph: Lucien Libert/Reuters</figcaption></span></figure></div></div></div><aside class="dcr-d7v0u9"><div class="dcr-m0fxig"><div class="dcr-1u8qly9"><span class="dcr-c2pk3s"></span><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/cybercrime" class="content__label__link dcr-1bcw2ie"><span>Cybercrime</span></a></div></div></aside><div class="dcr-am9ekn"><div class="dcr-6mja0y"><div class="dcr-19hyiet"><div class="dcr-1iguxsh"><a href="/tone/analysis" class="dcr-e5rynu">Analysis</a></div></div><h1 class="dcr-l0wuod">The French fridge hacks were relatively unsophisticated. An AI-enabled attack would raise the stakes</h1><div class="dcr-1ofjv3s"><div class="dcr-ogy8bq"><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/profile/danmilmo">Dan Milmo</a><span> Global technology editor</span></div></div></div></div><div class="dcr-usaq0w"><div class="dcr-3hhu73"><p>‘Not particularly complex’ defrosting hack has been attributed to Iran, with presumption it is linked to others across Europe</p></div></div><aside class="dcr-8c5fgk"><div class="dcr-u9k4zf"></div><div class="dcr-18aucbd"><div class="dcr-vyk1vu"><div class="dcr-19o5mbt"><div><details class="dcr-kzowyp"><summary class="dcr-njvsm3"><span class="dcr-10i63lj">Mon 9 Nov 2026 14.40 EST</span></summary>Last modified on Tue 10 Nov 2026 02.30 EST</details></div></div><div class="dcr-wk3oiu"><div class="dcr-h34qpb"><gu-island name="ShareButton" priority="feature" deferUntil="visible" props="{&quot;pageId&quot;:&quot;technology/2026/nov/09/cyber-attack-french-supermarket-freezers-iran-europe&quot;,&quot;webTitle&quot;:&quot;The French fridge hacks were relatively unsophisticated. An AI-enabled attack would raise the stakes&quot;,&quot;format&quot;:{&quot;design&quot;:6,&quot;display&quot;:0,&quot;theme&quot;:0},&quot;context&quot;:&quot;ArticleMeta&quot;}"><a href="mailto:?subject=The French fridge hacks were relatively unsophisticated. An AI-enabled attack would raise the stakes&amp;body=https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/09/cyber-attack-french-supermarket-freezers-iran-europe?CMP=share_btn_url" type="button" class="dcr-ncybeu">Share<div class="src-button-space"></div></a></gu-island></div><div class="dcr-6fc8w"><div class="dcr-1dx2i6k"><div></div></div></div></div></div></div></aside><div class="dcr-1s3g5r5"><div class="dcr-uwflda"><div id="maincontent" class="dcr-bnt37a"><div class="article-body-commercial-selector article-body-viewer-selector dcr-mb6w74"><p class="dcr-1s160rg">Iranian hackers like to send a message.</p><p class="dcr-1s160rg">In 2023 a state-backed group interrupted TV streaming services in the United Arab Emirates to broadcast a deepfake report on the war in Gaza. In the same year, hackers affiliated with Iran displayed the slogan “down with Israel” on the screens of <a href="https://www.sophos.com/en-us/blog/iranian-cyber-av3ngers-compromise-unitronics-systems">water-control equipment</a> made by an Israeli firm.</p><div id="sign-in-gate"></div><p class="dcr-1s160rg">Now France has attributed <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/09/france-blames-irans-revolutionary-guards-for-cyber-attacks-on-supermarket-fridges">an attack on supermarket fridges</a> to hackers operating under the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, a key branch of Iran’s military establishment.</p><p class="dcr-1s160rg">There was no message this time, but the disruption made headlines around the world. Some refrigerators had their temperature turned up so high they caught fire. ‌In the hours after the hack, supermarket employees dumped piles of perished meat and melted ice-cream into rubbish containers.</p><p class="dcr-1s160rg">France’s foreign minister described it as an act of “unprecedented scale and unprecedented aggression” and promised “Europe-wide sanctions” would be imposed against those responsible.</p><figure id="9c09dc0b-ad15-492e-9223-2c7b1849a11c" class="dcr-d9bay7"><div id="img-2" class="dcr-1t7hdmw"><a href="#img-2" class="open-lightbox dcr-zwabc0"><button type="button" class="open-lightbox dcr-1bitgpn"><span class="dcr-1shbixy">View image in fullscreen</span></button></a></div><figcaption class="dcr-174mzkf"><span class="dcr-vyhg7z"></span><span class="dcr-1cipnsy">An employee fills boxes of perished goods outside an Intermarche supermarket in Paris the day after the cyber-attack.</span> Photograph: Lucien Libert/Reuters</figcaption></figure><p class="dcr-1s160rg">It follows attacks on industrial freezers at an Israeli food production plant in May and a wave of freezer malfunctions that spoiled food at US military bases in August that are now reportedly being treated as a suspected hack. Those attacks have not been officially attributed to Iran, but the French announcement is the first to implicate the state in this type of hack.</p><figure class="dcr-d9bay7"><gu-island name="EmailSignUpWrapper" priority="feature" deferUntil="visible" props="{&quot;index&quot;:7,&quot;listId&quot;:6033,&quot;identityName&quot;:&quot;headlines-europe&quot;,&quot;category&quot;:&quot;fronts-based&quot;,&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A digest of the morning&#x27;s main headlines from the Europe edition emailed direct to you every week day&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Headlines Europe&quot;,&quot;frequency&quot;:&quot;Every day&quot;,&quot;theme&quot;:&quot;news&quot;,&quot;illustrationSquare&quot;:&quot;https://media.guim.co.uk/592014115340b327e81572e8ff5fe6b01ba4e717/0_0_3000_3000/500.jpg&quot;,&quot;exampleUrl&quot;:&quot;/email/europe/daily&quot;,&quot;idApiUrl&quot;:&quot;https://idapi.theguardian.com&quot;}"><a href="#EmailSignup-skip-link-7" class="dcr-76akua">skip past newsletter promotion</a><div class="dcr-1o2l3o8"><hr class="dcr-1cjdlyj"/><aside aria-label="newsletter promotion" class="dcr-1yp35al"><div class="dcr-ey8bdt"><span class="dcr-tzfgd8"></span><span class="dcr-14v327r"><span class="dcr-1a6mbo">Free newsletter |</span><span class="dcr-1a6mbo">Every day</span></span></div><hr class="dcr-1y8lc8t"/><div class="dcr-pspq5"><div class="dcr-1gx5ko4"><p class="dcr-vf9hps">Sign up to Headlines Europe</p><p class="dcr-djbwnz">A digest of the morning's main headlines from the Europe edition emailed direct to you every week day</p></div></div><form id="newsletter-signup-headlines-europe" class="dcr-taytpe"><div class="dcr-14fb2g8"><label for=":Rj3al52kdaf2kb:" class="dcr-0"><div class="dcr-5ay0jq">Enter your email </div></label><input type="email" id=":Rj3al52kdaf2kb:" required="" name="email" class="dcr-1xr8clv" value=""/></div><div class="dcr-dc6wbx"><button type="submit" class="dcr-72g8x5">Sign up</button></div></form></aside></div><p id="EmailSignup-skip-link-7" aria-label="after newsletter promotion" role="note" class="dcr-76akua">after newsletter promotion</p></gu-island></figure><figure id="052164e4-03a1-4707-b2dc-c8fd13eee32e" class="dcr-1oq85qr"><gu-island name="RichLinkComponent" priority="feature" deferUntil="idle" props="{&quot;richLinkIndex&quot;:8,&quot;element&quot;:{&quot;_type&quot;:&quot;model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.RichLinkBlockElement&quot;,&quot;prefix&quot;:&quot;Related: &quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Who is hacking Europe’s supermarket fridges and freezers, and why? &quot;,&quot;elementId&quot;:&quot;052164e4-03a1-4707-b2dc-c8fd13eee32e&quot;,&quot;role&quot;:&quot;richLink&quot;,&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/02/europe-supermarket-fridges-freezers-who-hacking-why&quot;},&quot;ajaxUrl&quot;:&quot;https://api.nextgen.guardianapps.co.uk&quot;,&quot;format&quot;:{&quot;design&quot;:6,&quot;display&quot;:0,&quot;theme&quot;:0}}"><div class="dcr-1rx5ky9"><div class="dcr-136okqj"><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/02/europe-supermarket-fridges-freezers-who-hacking-why" class="dcr-f35c0u"><div class="dcr-1qrpqn4"></div><div class="dcr-vvxpdt"><div class="dcr-6z6e7"><div class="dcr-zh9rih">Who is hacking Europe’s supermarket fridges and freezers, and why? </div></div><div class="dcr-oj13vq"><div class="dcr-ef9nua">Read more</div></div></div></a></div></div></gu-island></figure><p class="dcr-1s160rg">Rafe Pilling, a senior director at Sophos, a cybersecurity firm, said the relatively unsophisticated nature of the attack – hackers used default passwords to access fridge controls – was in line with Iranian capabilities. “The attacks themselves were not particularly complex,” he said.</p><p class="dcr-1s160rg">Pilling said there was now a presumption the French attacks and a wave of rogue defrosting hacks <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/02/supermarket-freezers-across-europe-hit-by-second-suspected-cyber-attack">across Europe last week</a> were linked.</p><p class="dcr-1s160rg">Hostile cyber operations against the west tend to come from a vexatious quartet of China, Russia, Iran and North Korea. All have different aims and characteristics: China has focused on industrial espionage and mass data gathering, Russia on disrupting enemy infrastructure and spreading disinformation, and North Korea on hacking crypto exchanges because it needs money. Iran, according to Pilling, wants to put a shot across the bow of its enemies.</p><figure id="764f841c-569d-48d6-8c2a-e7cefc116eb8" class="dcr-d9bay7"><div id="img-3" class="dcr-1t7hdmw"><a href="#img-3" class="open-lightbox dcr-zwabc0"><button type="button" class="open-lightbox dcr-1bitgpn"><span class="dcr-1shbixy">View image in fullscreen</span></button></a></div><figcaption class="dcr-174mzkf"><span class="dcr-vyhg7z"></span><span class="dcr-1cipnsy">Boxes of perished goods outside an Intermarche supermarket in Paris the day after the cyber-attack. </span> Photograph: Lucien Libert/Reuters</figcaption></figure><figure id="91df1d31-686e-4a18-a0d4-66e0207e4c9d" class="dcr-1oq85qr"><gu-island name="RichLinkComponent" priority="feature" deferUntil="idle" props="{&quot;richLinkIndex&quot;:13,&quot;element&quot;:{&quot;_type&quot;:&quot;model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.RichLinkBlockElement&quot;,&quot;prefix&quot;:&quot;Related: &quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Hot fridges, a fake nuclear alert and a $6bn heist: are we in the foothills of a hacking epidemic?&quot;,&quot;elementId&quot;:&quot;91df1d31-686e-4a18-a0d4-66e0207e4c9d&quot;,&quot;role&quot;:&quot;richLink&quot;,&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://www.theguardian.com/news/ng-interactive/2026/nov/08/hot-fridges-a-fake-nuclear-alert-and-a-6bn-heist-are-we-in-the-foothills-of-a-hacking-epidemic&quot;},&quot;ajaxUrl&quot;:&quot;https://api.nextgen.guardianapps.co.uk&quot;,&quot;format&quot;:{&quot;design&quot;:6,&quot;display&quot;:0,&quot;theme&quot;:0}}"><div class="dcr-1rx5ky9"><div class="dcr-136okqj"><a href="https://www.theguardian.com/news/ng-interactive/2026/nov/08/hot-fridges-a-fake-nuclear-alert-and-a-6bn-heist-are-we-in-the-foothills-of-a-hacking-epidemic" class="dcr-f35c0u"><div class="dcr-1qrpqn4"></div><div class="dcr-vvxpdt"><div class="dcr-6z6e7"><div class="dcr-zh9rih">Hot fridges, a fake nuclear alert and a $6bn heist: are we in the foothills of a hacking epidemic?</div></div><div class="dcr-oj13vq"><div class="dcr-ef9nua">Read more</div></div></div></a></div></div></gu-island></figure><p class="dcr-1s160rg">“In general Iran uses cyber-attacks to project power internationally and to signal their political displeasure with regional and international adversaries,” Pilling said.</p><p class="dcr-1s160rg">The fridge hacks came as the UK’s national cybersecurity centre warned the country is <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/news/ng-interactive/2026/nov/08/hot-fridges-a-fake-nuclear-alert-and-a-6bn-heist-are-we-in-the-foothills-of-a-hacking-epidemic">in the “foothills”</a> of a new level of cyber threat – and should expect a “huge rise” in the volume of incidents. The main concern is AI, a technology now capable of highlighting previously unforeseen flaws in IT systems and helping attackers plan and carry out attacks.</p><p class="dcr-1s160rg">However, the underlying context of the fridge attacks is not AI but geopolitics. After all, the refrigeration system vulnerabilities were relatively easy to find and exploit. But a combination of groundbreaking technology and tense global relations makes for an alarming landscape.</p></div></div><gu-island name="SlotBodyEnd" priority="feature" deferUntil="visible" props="{&quot;contentType&quot;:&quot;Article&quot;,&quot;contributionsServiceUrl&quot;:&quot;https://contributions.guardianapis.com&quot;,&quot;idApiUrl&quot;:&quot;https://idapi.theguardian.com&quot;,&quot;isPaidContent&quot;:false,&quot;pageId&quot;:&quot;technology/2026/nov/09/cyber-attack-french-supermarket-freezers-iran-europe&quot;,&quot;sectionId&quot;:&quot;technology&quot;,&quot;shouldHideReaderRevenue&quot;:false,&quot;tags&quot;:[{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;technology/cybercrime&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Cybercrime&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;technology/hacking&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Hacking&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;technology/artificialintelligenceai&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;Keyword&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;AI (artificial intelligence)&quot;},{&quot;id&quot;:&quot;t